# COPY THIS INTO YOUR REPO ROOT ALONGSIDE factory/, OR MERGE IT INTO THE ONE YOU HAVE.
#
# This is not housekeeping. Two different things break without it.
#
# 1. THE FACTORY WILL NOT START. `run-workflow.sh` runs a pre-flight before any node that
#    can commit, and it REFUSES to run when a credential-shaped path is not ignored:
#
#        ESCALATE: PREFLIGHT: .env is not gitignored; a git add -A would publish it
#
#    That check is right, and it fires whether or not the file exists yet - because the
#    danger is the file that appears for the first time three weeks from now, inside a
#    `git add -A` in a step nobody is watching. On a public repo that is publication, and
#    rotating afterwards is the cleanup, not the fix.
#
# 2. THE FACTORY'S OWN ARTIFACTS WOULD BE COMMITTED. Every run writes rendered prompts,
#    node stdout, plans and verdicts under `.factory/runs/`. Those are BUILDER artifacts.
#    Committing them puts the implementer's plan on the branch, where the validator can
#    read it - which quietly destroys the holdout property the whole auto-merge argument
#    rests on. The tripwire exists to catch that; this stops it happening.

# --- credentials. The pre-flight asserts these, so removing a line stops the factory ---
.env
.env.*
secrets.json
credentials.json
*.local
*credential*
*secret*
*.pem
*.key
id_rsa
id_ed25519
service-account.json

# Agent and tool config that routinely carries a live token. Add your own tool's here;
# the point is the shape, not this exact list.
.archon/config.yaml
.archon/.env
.claude/settings.local.json
config/secrets.yml

# --- factory runtime state: per-machine, regenerated, never meaningful in history ---
.factory/runs/
.factory/locks-runtime/
.factory/STOP
factory-*.log

# The validator's findings, handed from gate.sh to the fix node across two processes.
# Runtime, regenerated on every red verdict - and BUILDER-VISIBLE by design, so it must
# never reach a branch: committed, it would put the judge's reasoning in the tree the next
# validator reads.
.factory/findings/

# Every lap creates one of these. `run-workflow.sh` adds `.worktrees/i<n>` for the
# implementer and `.worktrees/v<n>` for the validator, and a crashed run can leave the
# directory behind - `git worktree remove --force` drops git's record and still leaves
# the files on disk, routinely, on Windows. Untracked and unignored, they turn up in the
# guard's working-tree union and in anyone's `git add -A`, and what they contain is a
# full second copy of the repository at another commit.
.worktrees/

# The runner's own modules generate these next to factory/*.py.
__pycache__/
*.pyc

# --- deploy snapshots: rebuildable from any sha ---
builds/

# NOT ignored, deliberately:
#   .factory/locks/    the thresholds a human set. Protected, and tracked on purpose -
#                      a floor that is not in history cannot be shown not to have moved.
#   .factory/holdout/  tracked while it lives in-repo. Moving it to a sibling repo is
#                      the upgrade; deleting it from history is not.
#   .factory/prs/      the PR record on the file backend IS state, so it is tracked.
